! Parked for `lares(Codex)` — a question and two named reds

''Closed.'' Every item below landed, and the Codex hold that kept this row off the crew's own ledger stands closed (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/crew-handoff-codex#/reconciled">>). EOT2 retired as a frame mark; the web oracle panel and the scenario harness typecheck clean (`e9d6ddefe`, `3e8847364`, and `tools/typecheck-witness.sh` across every project); the staged joinee boot reads the lease frontier its own daemon layer folds (`a59d9a9b5`); `held-out.yml` computes its evidence path inside a step (`00c679e97`). The sections below keep the row as it was parked.

''Why this carrier, not the crew's own ledger.'' `bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/crew-handoff-codex.mem`
sits on the Codex Lares hearth's held ground (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths#/holds`, trailer
`session_01a0a6f209517170ad254cf14549cc5f`). This hand carries no crossing row for that ground, so the
house pattern parks the row here instead — a `docs/handback-*.mem` carrier the crew (or whoever opens the
next crossing) folds in on its own schedule.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/eot2-question>>

!! EOT2 on `kapu.mem` — an open question, not a ruling

''Closed beside the record:'' every carrier closes on plain EOT, kapu-tier included (`memetic-wikitext-framing.mem`, the SOH₂ paragraph), and `kapu.mem` closes on `&#x0004;`; no EOT2 frames any tier. The question below stands as asked.

''Context, not law — stated once so the question reads clean.'' The relay/stream ontology stands
mid-refactor; the operator's working frame reads EOT2 as transport framing: EOT2 serves STREAMED text or
encoded memes BETWEEN RELAYS, never an at-rest carrier's own close. Nothing below writes that frame into
canon — the framing spec (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext-framing`) stays silent on EOT2
until the refactor lands.

''The question.'' `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapu` ends on an authored EOT2 close
(`<<^ code="&#x0014;" -> to="?">>`), a choice `d207b7ef0` ("refuse escaped meme tails") made by REMOVING
the canonical EOT line that stood beside it, leaving EOT2 standing alone. Read against the transport
framing above: an at-rest carrier closes on EOT; EOT2 names a relay-stream boundary. If that framing
holds, the EOT2 line on `kapu.mem` reads as the escaped tail the same commit meant to teach a reader to
refuse — or the crew held a specific reason to keep it that this hand does not carry. Which one?

''What a fresh hand hits either way.'' The emitter re-mints canonical EOT on normalize, so the file as it
stands today CANNOT round-trip: a normalize pass restamps EOT2 back to EOT, and `8fb7fe383` already had
to hand-restore the EOT2 line once after exactly that happened. Whoever answers the question above should
also say whether the emitter needs a carve-out for an authored EOT2 close, or whether `kapu.mem` should
simply close on EOT like every other carrier.

''Ruled (operator, 2026-10-04):'' `kapu.mem` closes on regular EOT for now, like every other carrier
at rest. No emitter carve-out. EOT2's office stays open until the relay-stream layer settles.

''Superseding ruling (operator, 2026-10-04):'' EOT2 (`&#x0014;`) is RETIRED as a frame mark outright —
the question above resolves, not by settling a relay-stream office for it, but by removing the mark.
Every carrier closes on plain EOT, at rest and in flight; stream/batch framing belongs to a future
transport envelope and gets its own mark when that envelope lands, never a repurposed EOT2. SYN
(`&#x0016;`) stays the one reserved stream mark; SOH2 (`&#x0011;`) is the ONLY kapu alias this grammar
stands. Dropped from `packages/lararium-memetic-frame/src/marks.ts` (FRAME_MARKS), the
`sigil-frame-eot2.tid` tiddler (deleted, grammar-table regenerated), the control-set table and TOML
schema rows and trust-tier range in this grammar's own framing law, and the bootstrap scanner's DC4
scan (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/meme-ast/scanner.ts`). `marks.ts`'s `frameHex`/`frameAlt` also moved
off NAME-PREFIX family matching (which let `"EOT2".startsWith("EOT")` alias it into EOT's own readers
with nothing declaring that) onto an explicit `FAMILY_ALIASES` table — SOH2's alias into SOH stays
explicit there; no family aliases silently by spelling again.

<<~/ahu>>

